<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Weimar Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1933 CE · capital Berlin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's first parliamentary democracy, born of defeat in 1918 and destroyed by depression and the rise of the Nazis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> collapsed in November 1918 with naval mutinies and revolution. On 9 November <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a> proclaimed a republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">William II</a> abdicated. A national assembly met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> to avoid unrest in the capital and adopted a democratic constitution in August 1919, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a>, political murders and the hyperinflation of 1923, when the French occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-stresemann">Gustav Stresemann</a> it stabilised, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and saw a cultural flowering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> to cinema. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> brought mass unemployment and rule by emergency decree from 1930.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> chancellor on 30 January 1933. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> of February 1933 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933">Enabling Act</a> of March 1933 destroyed parliamentary rule, ending the republic and beginning <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/weimar-republic.jpg" alt="Weimar Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User:Mmxx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany_(3-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1933 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Berlin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Friedrich Ebert, Gustav Stresemann, Paul von Hindenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>German Reich (1918-1933), Deutsches Reich</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=weimar-republic" aria-label="Weimar Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4980 366 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Weimar Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5300 622L5300 622M5300 622L5269 749M5300 622L5269 749M5300 622L5300 622M5300 622L5312 664M5300 622L5250 665M5300 622L5275 669M5300 622L5276 641M5300 622L5198 698M5300 622L5212 730M5300 622L5194 723M5300 622L5237 629M5300 622L5183 604M5300 622L5200 638M5300 622L5203 629M5300 622L5204 655M5300 622L5254 591M5300 622L5291 598M5300 622L5197 606M5300 622L5300 622M5300 622L5227 665M5300 622L5179 693M5300 622L5161 717M5300 622L5139 672"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5269 749h0M5269 749h0M5300 622h0M5312 664h0M5250 665h0M5275 669h0M527